```python
from itertools import combinations

def three_ap_free(A):
    s = set(A)
    return not any((a + c) % 2 == 0 and (a + c) // 2 in s
                   for a, c in combinations(sorted(s), 2))

def roth_number(N):
    for k in range(N, -1, -1):
        if any(three_ap_free(S) for S in combinations(range(N), k)):
            return k
```

This brute force examines up to $2^N$ subsets, which is why the lesson's experiment uses backtracking with pruning to reach $N = 40$. Note that for distinct $a \lt c$ the midpoint $(a + c)/2$ is automatically different from both, so the test only finds genuine progressions.
